# PATTERN_UNPORTED -- 17 rows. THE SEVENTEEN `srneg` ROWS, AND THE FINDING IS THAT EVERY ONE OF THEM IS A DIVERGENCE IN THIS CLASS. `wgsl.bend` carries wgsl.py:65-93's seventeen rule BODIES and none of their `UPat`s, so the port has no predicate that could decline a node: each `srneg` row drives a body on a node CPython's own `UPat.match` REJECTS (the oracle ASSERTS the rejection at the source, so the row is a refusal by construction) and the port answers anyway. CARVE-OUT, and the right one: this is not a wrong ANSWER, it is an absent REACHABILITY CHECK, and pinning it is what stops the seventeen `sr` rows from being read as seventeen branch proofs. TWO OF THE SEVENTEEN ARE DANGEROUS RATHER THAN COSMETIC and they are the ones to read first. `srneg 14 store u8` -- a PACKED store of an ordinary CONST -- is not a read-modify-write, `packed_rmw`'s `AND` is the whole of rule 14's pattern, and `sr_rmw` answers `atomicAnd(&B[1],W);` and DROPS THE STORED VALUE. `srneg 15 store 3` -- a GATED store, three srcs -- falls to rule 15 and `sr_store` answers `B = V;` and DROPS THE GATE. Both are unreachable from `render_kernel` as `wgsl.bend` currently drives it, and both would be a silently wrong shader if the dispatcher ever handed them these nodes. The other fifteen answer a string where CPython declines, which is unreachable in the same way and is the same absence.
